# Apps Web Svartz Runtime

`apps/web` is the static SvelteKit shell that consumes Svartz runtime surfaces, but the real `@svartz/vite` plugin is injected by the CLI at dev/build time.

- Do not add `@svartz/vite` as a direct `apps/web` dependency just to get virtual-module typings.
- Do not add `@svartz/core` as a direct `apps/web` dependency just to import shared runtime types.
- `@svartz/ui/runtime` owns `SvartzRuntimePage` and its `virtual:svartz/*` declarations. The app references those declarations from `src/app.d.ts`.
- Route files remain in the app. They pass the current pathname into the package renderer, so the library does not depend on SvelteKit's page state.
- For browser/unit tests, keep the `virtual:svartz/*` test surfaces file-backed under `apps/web/src/lib/svartz/testing/fixtures/` and alias them from `apps/web/vite.config.ts`. The host registry fixture points to the theme and artifact fixtures. Do not go back to giant inline string-literal virtual modules.
- Do not import `@svartz/vite` or call `svartz()` in `apps/web/vite.config.ts`; production/dev injection belongs to the CLI. The shell may load an opaque CLI-generated plugin module from `SVARTZ_VITE_PLUGINS_MODULE_PATH` so SvelteKit's secondary build retains the standalone CSS collector.
- When the CLI moves `kit.outDir` outside `apps/web` into `.svartz/vaults/<id>/.svelte-kit`, it must also prepare that vault workspace so the generated prerender server can still resolve `apps/web` dependencies. Today that means ensuring `.svartz/vaults/<id>/node_modules` points at `apps/web/node_modules`.
